  keeps your earlier storage choice reversible.

  [1mNew — [38;5;153mtracker.js[39m (~15 lines).[0m The only thing that emits user-attributable console output. Exposes [38;5;153mTracker.log(event, [39m
  [38;5;153mdetail)[39m, which reads the current id from [38;5;153mAppSession[39m and stamps it onto every line, logging [38;5;153manonymous[39m when there is
  none. Call sites never pass a userId and therefore can never forget one.

  [1mChanged — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.[0m Two script tags before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, in dependency order: [38;5;153msession.js[39m, [38;5;153mtracker.js[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Still
  classic scripts; [38;5;153mfile://[39m still works.

  [1mChanged — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns the server-issued id alongside its existing fields; the submit handler hands that id
  to [38;5;153mAppSession[39m and then calls [38;5;153mTracker.log[39m. [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m is untouched.

  [1mThe thing to object to if you're going to object:[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m does [1mnot[0m get a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. Its signature stays
  [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m. That's a direct consequence of your "server-issued, post-auth" answer — the id doesn't
  exist until [38;5;153mlogin[39m has run, so it can only be a return value. If you actually wanted a caller-chosen id passed [3min[0m,
  that's a different identifier than the one we've been designing, and I'd want to back up.

  Alongside the architecture, a tooling question: this repo has no linter, no formatter, and no test runner —
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has zero dependencies and zero scripts. Setting any of that up is cheapest now, before the code exists,
  and whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints so the implementation plan inherits it.

